--TEST--
a `HEAD` request matches the `Get` route the table holds, `Core\Request::method` reports `Get`, and `isHead` is the only member carrying the difference — `rule:http-server/head-runs-as-get`, `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:http-server/head-runs-as-get`'s first convention, asserted from inside the program rather
// than at the door: a `HEAD` *is* a `Get` whose body is dropped, so a table
// declaring only `Get` must still match one. Reporting `Head` would fail the
// application's own `Core\Router::match` against a `Get` row and produce the
// `404` the convention exists to prevent, which is why the verb is gone by the
// time it is a case and `isHead` is the one member that can still see it.
//
// The depth shape is *agreement*: three readings of one request line are asked
// in one breath — the match the door took, the case `method()` answers and the
// token `isHead` compares — so a member that grew its own reading of the verb
// disagrees here while still printing plausibly on its own line.
//
// **The branch is the request**: `Core\Test::request` runs this same file
// again to answer it, and `Core\Env::context()` is `Request` in that run
// (`rule:core-classes/env-context`). The child is the `if` branch.
//
// The server's half of the convention holds too: `Core\Test::request` answers
// a `HEAD` the way the server does, with no body (`rule:testing/in-process-request`).
// So the child reports its three readings in a header, which a `HEAD` keeps,
// and the parent checks that the body it echoed is gone.
class Pages {
    // `rule:attributes/access-is-a-required-sibling`: the access decision is a required sibling of every
    // `#[Route]`. Only `Get` is declared, and that is the point.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/page", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(): string { return "shown"; }
}

if (Core\Env::context() == Core\Env::Context::Request) {
    // The child. `route()` is the match `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler` took before this isolate
    // existed, and a `Get`-only table claiming a `HEAD` request is the whole
    // convention.
    var $matched = Core\Request::route();
    var $seen = "";
    if ($matched == null) {
        $seen = "unmatched";
    } else {
        $seen = $matched->is(Pages::show) ? "Pages::show" : "another route";
    }
    // `rule:enums/no-class-machinery`: a case is its integer constant, so the comparison is
    // between two constants and never between two spellings.
    var $reported = Core\Request::method() as int;
    if ($reported == Core\Http\Method::Get as int) {
        $seen = $seen . " method=Get";
    } else {
        $seen = $seen . " method=other";
    }
    // The truth the parse threw away, back from the one member that kept it.
    if (Core\Request::isHead()) {
        $seen = $seen . " isHead=true";
    } else {
        $seen = $seen . " isHead=false";
    }
    Core\Response::setHeader("X-Seen", $seen);
    echo $seen;
} else {
    var $answered = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Head, "/page");
    echo "status ", $answered->status(), "\n";
    echo "seen ", $answered->header("X-Seen") ?? "none", "\n";
    echo "body [", $answered->body(), "]\n";
}
--EXPECT--
status 200
seen Pages::show method=Get isHead=true
body []
